<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A1.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A1.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T5.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="A1.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T5.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How it works</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="A1.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T5.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Advantages</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="A1.T5.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="A1.T5.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T5.2.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Disadvantages</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A1.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T5.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct elicitation</span> (ask the model “What is <math id="A1.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(B\mid A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(B\mid A)</annotation></semantics></math>?”)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Prompt the model to output a numeric probability for <math id="A1.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> under the condition <math id="A1.T5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> (optionally after reasoning)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_align_left ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<span id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uses global semantics &amp; latent knowledge; can consider <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> holistically rather than token-by-token</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Supports reasoning (e.g., via CoT) before committing to a number.</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Black-box friendly: no logit access required</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, verbalized probabilities can be well-calibrated—often better than token-likelihoods for instruct/chat models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2023</a>)</cite></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="A1.T5.2.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_align_left ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<span id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Less decomposable/explainable at the score level (gives a number, not a token-level breakdown)</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Variance under sampling (for non-greedy decoding)</span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="A1.T5.2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sentence probability</span> (likelihood of text <math id="A1.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> given text <math id="A1.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:62.1pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Compute <math id="A1.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(B\mid A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(B\mid A)</annotation></semantics></math> by multiplying (summing log) token probabilities of the exact string <math id="A1.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> under left-to-right factorization</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_align_left ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<span id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Well-defined and deterministic (given fixed model &amp; settings)</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Decomposable at token level; supports log-likelihood ratios and ablations.</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Aligns with the model’s pre-training objective (good for base models)</span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A1.T5.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block">
<span id="A1.T5.2.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_align_left ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A1.I4" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<span id="A1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measures string form, not pure semantics; highly paraphrase- and tokenization-sensitive</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Length bias (longer <math id="A1.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> penalized); normalization choices matter (per-token, per-byte, etc.)</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Not directly comparable across models without normalization (different tokenizers, logit scales, priors)</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Requires logit access</span>
</span></span>
<span id="A1.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="A1.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A1.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Can’t natively leverage the model’s reasoning (e.g., via CoT) during scoring</span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Comparison of methods for eliciting (conditional) probability judgments with large language models.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompts</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the vanilla prompts follow a zero-shot format, providing only the task instruction, the scenario context, and the input item, the few-shot variants additionally include three example judgments based on a shared narrative context (see Figure <a href="#A1.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ A.7 ANOVA Tables ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ If Probable, Then Acceptable? Understanding Conditional Acceptability Judgments in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). These examples span a range of output values (high, medium, low) to illustrate the rating scale. The same context and examples are used across all judgment types, with minor numerical variation to avoid repetition. Relation types are omitted to avoid bias.
All prompts are constructed using each model’s respective chat formatting template. Instruction-tuned LLMs typically expect input messages to be wrapped in a specific format. These templates are applied using each model’s tokenizer. Where applicable, the model’s default system prompt is used. Model outputs are generated using the default decoding parameters provided by the Hugging Face Transformers library, with nucleus sampling (<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="top\_p=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">top\_p=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) and temperature (<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Outputs</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each model judgment is expected to be a numerical value between <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">completely improbable or unacceptable</em>) and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">highly probable or acceptable</em>). To elicit consistent responses, all prompts explicitly instruct models to return a single numerical value using both a textual instruction (<em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Respond only with this number and nothing else.</em>) and a predefined output format (<em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Answer: &lt;number&gt;</em>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A1.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
